但1912年的公寓禁令阻止了开发商建造各种公寓。既包括像这样为城市富裕和时尚居民准备的高档昂贵场所，也包括适合中产阶级家庭的经济型中产住宅。斯帕迪纳花园并没有引发一股流氓开发商无视附例、到处建造公寓楼的潮流，因为在6061号附例通过后，城市规划者并没有结束他们对公寓楼的战争。随着多伦多在60年代向外扩张，城市规划者将住宅区指定为“不可侵犯”，意味着它们不能受到新开发项目的影响。

25
00:13:44.000 --> 00:14:16.000
The only thing you can build there are single-family homes. It was a real statement of do not mess with our neighborhoods. On the city land use map, these in-violet neighborhoods were coloured in yellow. That's why for years Toronto has had something called the Yellow Belt. A sea of neighborhoods where new development was limited to detached single-family homes. According to House Devited, a book about Toronto's missing middle, the yellow belt in Toronto is more than twice the size of Manhattan. This yellow belt zoning stops Toronto from building more middle housing.
你唯一能在那儿建造的就是单户住宅。这确实是一种“别动我们的社区”的声明。在城市土地利用地图上，这些不可侵犯的社区被涂成了黄色。这就是为什么多年来多伦多一直有所谓的“黄带区”。一片海洋般的社区，新开发项目仅限于独立的单户住宅。根据关于多伦多“缺失的中产住宅”的书籍《分裂的房屋》，多伦多的黄带区面积是曼哈顿的两倍多。这种黄带区分区阻止了多伦多建造更多的中产住宅。

26
00:14:16.000 --> 00:14:55.000
That's why we don't find a duplex or a low rise walkup on every corner. And that missing middle in Toronto has real consequences for the city. Urbanists say the lack of middle housing in Toronto has led to a divided city. If all you build are single-family homes, that makes lots of residential neighborhoods unaffordable. Historically, middle housing has been disproportionately useful for immigrant families and single women. Apartments provided women with the opportunity to access affordable housing more independently. So with single attached housing or even semi-attached housing, a woman will only be able to access that if they were living as a domestic servant or if they were a wife.
这就是为什么我们在每个街角都找不到复式公寓或低层步梯公寓。多伦多缺失的中产住宅对城市产生了真正的影响。城市规划者表示，多伦多缺乏中产住宅导致了城市的分裂。如果你只建造单户住宅，那会使许多住宅区变得难以负担。从历史上看，中产住宅对移民家庭和单身女性特别有用。公寓为女性提供了更独立地获得经济适用房的机会。因此，对于单户住宅甚至半独立式住宅，女性只有在作为家庭佣人或妻子的情况下才能获得。

27
00:14:55.000 --> 00:15:17.000
Cheryl Case is one of the editors of the book House Divided. She says the missing middle has led to economic but also racial segregation in Toronto. So the missing middle has always been a race issue. So for evidence of this, you can actually look back into Thorncrest Village, which is a exclusively detached neighborhood that was built in Atobico. The neighborhood Cheryl is talking about
谢丽尔·凯斯是《分裂的房屋》一书的编辑之一。她说，“缺失的中产住宅”导致了多伦多的经济和种族隔离。所以，“缺失的中产住宅”一直是一个种族问题。关于这一点，你可以回顾一下索恩克雷斯特村，这是一个建在怡陶碧谷的纯独立住宅区。谢丽尔所说的社区

28
00:15:17.000 --> 00:15:46.000
was one of the first real suburban communities built in Toronto in the 1940s. But it could be almost anywhere in the yellow belt. It's large, single-family homes surrounded by trees and greenery. You wouldn't know you're in a huge North American metropolis. And so in this neighborhood, people had to apply to buy housing there, and the people who were applying explained why they were applying to buy housing there, and they very directly explained that they were buying that housing,
是20世纪40年代在多伦多建造的首批真正的郊区社区之一。但它几乎可以是黄带区的任何地方。它有大型的单户住宅，周围环绕着树木和绿地。你不会知道自己身处一个巨大的北美大都市。在这个社区，人们必须申请才能购买住房，而申请的人解释了他们为什么要申请购买那里的住房，他们非常直接地解释说，他们购买那里的住房，

29
00:15:46.000 --> 00:16:16.000
so they could escape the diversity of the other neighborhoods. Zoning might have been designed to protect these neighborhoods from change, but Cheryl says that even they suffer, while the city as a whole grows, these neighborhoods are actually losing population. It's a part of a broader picture, right, about living healthily and having a healthy communities. So right now, on the city of Toronto, you have schools closing all across the city, because these are neighborhoods that haven't seen any growth happening in them.
是为了逃避其他社区的多样性。分区法的设计初衷可能是为了保护这些社区免受变化的影响，但谢丽尔说，即使是它们也受到了损害。当整个城市都在增长时，这些社区的人口实际上在减少。这是一个更宏大图景的一部分，对吧，关于健康生活和拥有健康的社区。所以现在，在多伦多市，学校在全市范围内关闭，因为这些社区没有看到任何增长。

30
00:16:16.000 --> 00:17:07.000
You might assume a lot of the city's housing woes could be explained by a lack of new construction. But anyone who lives in Toronto knows the city is building lots of giant condo towers. In fact, between 1996 and 2016, Toronto built housing at one and a half times the rate of population growth. In theory, these condos should provide more housing and bring prices down. And I have to think that it helps a little, but these new condos haven't fixed the city's housing lows. Toronto is still way behind in terms of housing availability and the legacy of banning most buildings that would have made up the missing middle is partially to blame. A report found that in 2020, Toronto had 360 housing units for every 1000 residents. That's well below the average for cities in G7 countries, which is 471 units for every 1000
你可能会认为城市许多住房问题可以用缺乏新建筑来解释。但任何住在多伦多的人都知道，这座城市正在建造许多巨大的公寓塔楼。事实上，在1996年至2016年间，多伦多的住房建设速度是人口增长速度的1.5倍。理论上，这些公寓应该提供更多住房并降低价格。我不得不认为这有一点帮助，但这些新公寓并没有解决城市的住房低迷问题。多伦多在住房供应方面仍然远远落后，而禁止大多数本可以构成“缺失的中产住宅”的建筑的遗留问题是部分原因。一份报告发现，2020年，多伦多每1000名居民拥有360套住房。这远低于G7国家城市每1000名居民拥有471套住房的平均水平。

31
00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:50.000
residents. And those units that do exist in Toronto aren't bringing prices down. A one-bedroom apartment there rents for over $2,000 a month, and that number keeps going up. These units are not putting downward pressure on the price of a detached house either, which is around $2 million. For me, a big problem with these new condo towers is this. They only really serve a very narrow range of people. Most of the new residences being built are luxury. I hope you can hear the air quotes there. Luxury condos that only have one bedroom and are totally unsuitable for families. They also come with hefty monthly fees that make them unaffordable for lots of people.
而多伦多现有的那些单元并没有降低价格。那里的一居室公寓租金超过每月2000美元，而且这个数字还在不断上涨。这些单元也没有对独立屋的价格产生下行压力，独立屋的价格约为200万加元。对我来说，这些新公寓塔楼的一个大问题是：它们实际上只服务于极少数人。大多数正在建造的新住宅都是豪华的——希望你能听到那里的引号——只有一间卧室且完全不适合家庭居住的豪华公寓。它们还附带高昂的月费，使许多人负担不起。

32
00:17:50.000 --> 00:18:12.000
Those small units only serve one type of market. Young, single professionals who want a clean, low effort place to live. Anyone else is kind of crowded out, and in the current market, that's all that gets built. I spoke to Nama Blonder. She's an architect and a planner, and she runs a company called Smart Density. She's all about the missing middle.
那些小单元只服务于一种市场：想要一个干净、低维护居住场所的年轻单身专业人士。其他人基本上被挤出去了，而在当前的市场中，这就是唯一会被建造的东西。我采访了娜玛·布隆德。她是一位建筑师和规划师，经营着一家名为“智能密度”的公司。她非常关注“缺失的中产住宅”。

33
00:18:12.000 --> 00:18:41.000
She wants the city to be filling in those density gaps. I do believe condos, they belong in the city. They belong next to expensive infrastructure, such as transit. I have nothing against them. Yet, it seems like we're building a lot of units and they don't reflect the diversity that is currently needed in the city. When Namah says diversity, here,
她希望城市能填补这些密度缺口。我相信公寓属于城市。它们属于昂贵的基础设施旁边，比如交通枢纽。我并不反对它们。然而，似乎我们建造了很多单元，但它们并没有反映出城市目前所需的多样性。当娜玛在这里说“多样性”时，

34
00:18:41.000 --> 00:19:01.000
she's referencing the types of units that are built. I've been scouring floor plans for proposed and in-progress developments, and they're largely tiny one bedrooms or bachelor's and studios. I want to call it misguided, but it's not really guided at all. Developers just can't build much else.
她指的是所建造单元的类型。我一直在搜寻拟建和在建项目的平面图，它们大多是微小的单间公寓、单身公寓和工作室。我想称之为“误导”，但实际上根本没有引导。开发商只是无法建造其他东西。

35
00:19:01.000 --> 00:19:28.000
The thing is there are developers who want to build middle housing, but in most places, it's still illegal to build anything except for single-family homes, and in the places where you can put up taller buildings, all the incentives push you in the direction of giant condo towers. Jason Allen John is a mortgage broker and a developer in Toronto. He wants to build affordable housing in communities he cares about, like Toronto's Little Jamaican neighborhood.
问题在于，有开发商想要建造中产住宅，但在大多数地方，除了单户住宅外，建造任何东西仍然是非法的；而在你可以建造更高建筑的地方，所有的激励措施都把你推向巨大的公寓塔楼。杰森·艾伦·约翰是多伦多的一名抵押贷款经纪人和开发商。他想在他关心的社区（如多伦多的小牙买加社区）建造经济适用房。

36
00:19:28.000 --> 00:19:59.000
You know, like my personal vision would be kind of cool if you turn into a massive, like business commerce hub for, you know, black individuals. Like, I think significant missing middle towers, a mix of condos, affordable ownership, commercial space, office buildings, all on that strip. Jason bought a site in Little Jamaica a couple of years ago. He wanted to kickstart the process of revitalizing the area by providing affordable mid-rise apartment buildings.
你知道，我的个人愿景是，如果你能把它变成一个巨大的商业中心，为黑人个人服务，那会很酷。比如，我认为显著的“缺失的中产住宅”塔楼，混合了公寓、经济适用房所有权、商业空间、办公楼，都在那条街上。杰森几年前在小牙买加买了一块地。他想通过提供经济适用的中层公寓楼来启动该地区的振兴进程。

37
00:19:59.000 --> 00:20:50.000
So I wanted to say, oh no, you can do a project. That is consumer submittal and you can do a project. That is concert misimidial. And you can make a little, you can make some money. You can provide jobs for individuals and then also inspire people to do this themselves. But Jason's dream may never become a reality. Jason says every time he tries to get financing for a low-rise apartment complex, he's told by investors to build condos or single family homes instead. I've had to do a lot of convincing on the project itself because, you know, most people will tell me just do a nice single family dwelling and build it and sell it for 2.5 million or something, right? So that's kind of where everybody wants you to go. So it's like, no matter where you turn in a sense, people will be like, oh no, why don't you just do this? Even if you do get the financing, the approval process can take
所以我想说，哦不，你可以做一个项目。这是一个消费者提交的项目，你可以做一个项目。这是一个音乐会中产住宅项目。你可以赚点钱。你可以为个人提供就业机会，然后激励人们自己做这件事。但杰森的梦想可能永远不会成为现实。杰森说，每次他试图为低层公寓大楼融资时，投资者都会告诉他去建公寓或单户住宅。我必须在项目本身上做很多说服工作，因为你知道，大多数人会告诉我，只管建一个漂亮的单户住宅，建好后以250万左右的价格卖掉，对吧？所以这就是每个人都想让你去的地方。所以无论你转向哪里，人们都会说，哦不，你为什么不直接做这个呢？即使你确实获得了融资，审批过程也可能需要

38
00:20:50.000 --> 00:21:41.000
years and there are incredibly high development fees. In fact, the city just proposed an increase to those fees of 49%. If it takes the same amount of time to get a hundred unit building approved as it does a ten unit building and there are huge fees associated with it either way, that destroys the already narrow profit margin on the smaller building. A bigger project has a bigger margin and can swallow the fees and delays more easily. Politicians in Ontario have done almost nothing to address this. The provincial government led by Doug Ford recently introduced a housing bill, but it ignores single-family zoning entirely. That might change. City planners have just drafted an amendment to allow up to a four-plex in protected neighborhoods, but it's a pretty conservative plan
几年时间，而且开发费用高得惊人。事实上，市政府刚刚提议将这些费用提高49%。如果批准一栋100个单元的建筑和批准一栋10个单元的建筑所需的时间相同，而且无论哪种方式都有巨大的相关费用，那就会摧毁小型建筑本已微薄的利润空间。更大的项目有更大的利润空间，可以更容易地消化费用和延误。安大略省的政客们几乎没有采取任何措施来解决这个问题。道格·福特领导的省政府最近提出了一项住房法案，但它完全忽略了单户住宅分区。这可能会改变。城市规划者刚刚起草了一项修正案，允许在受保护的社区建造最多四联排公寓，但这只是一个相当保守的计划，

39
00:21:41.000 --> 00:21:59.000
with lots of caveats, and it's a long way from reaching the city council for approval. Of course Toronto isn't the only city with this missing middle problem. In fact, other cities with a missing middle have tried to take concrete steps to fix it. Cities like Portland, Oregon, which in recent decades has tried to make the city
有很多附加条件，而且距离提交市议会批准还有很长的路要走。当然，多伦多并不是唯一一个有“缺失的中产住宅”问题的城市。事实上，其他有“缺失的中产住宅”的城市已经尝试采取具体步骤来解决它。比如俄勒冈州的波特兰市，近几十年来一直试图让城市

40
00:21:59.000 --> 00:22:29.000
more livable for renters. Camille Trummer worked with the mayor of Portland to pass zoning reform. And in the process, she became public enemy number one for NIMBies, the people who don't want new apartment developments in their neighborhood. There were campaigns cropping up in different neighborhoods across the city that were really if we want to be transparent, kind of firmly rooted in NIMBYism, not in my backyard. What do you think they were scared of?
对租房者更友好。卡米尔·特鲁默与波特兰市长合作通过了分区改革。在这个过程中，她成了NIMBY（邻避主义者）的头号公敌，这些人不希望在他们的社区有新的公寓开发项目。在城市各处的不同社区中，确实出现了一些运动，如果我们想透明一点的话，它们实际上是坚定地植根于NIMBY主义——“不要在我家后院”。你认为他们害怕什么？

41
00:22:29.000 --> 00:22:50.000
To be honest with you, Jay, I think they were scared of low-income people moving into their neighborhoods and whatever serial types or narratives they've built through media consumption and lack of education and lack of interaction with people that are different than them, about who they believe those people are and what they would do to their neighborhoods.
老实说，杰伊，我认为他们害怕低收入人群搬进他们的社区，以及通过媒体消费、缺乏教育和缺乏与不同于他们的人的互动，他们所建立的关于他们认为这些人是谁以及他们会对他们的社区做什么的任何刻板印象或叙事。

42
00:22:50.000 --> 00:23:47.000
Camille says Portland used to have the same exclusionary zoning laws as Toronto, and changing them was a real uphill fight for Mayor Charlie Hales. And what was so ironic is that many of Charlie's neighbors were the ones and the people most vocal in terms of the Nimbism group. So that was really challenging for Charlie to be unable to even talk with his neighbors about his vision. And so we saw a lot of manifestations of narratives and small campaigns advocating for status quo. To change the law, Camille had to build a coalition of people herself, people who weren't wealthy homeowners. So, she went out in the community and gathered herself a steering committee that looked a lot more like Portland. It was such a visual kind of challenge for me
卡米尔说，波特兰过去和多伦多有同样的排他性分区法，改变它们对查理·黑尔斯市长来说是一场真正的艰苦斗争。具有讽刺意味的是，查理的许多邻居正是那些在邻避主义群体中最直言不讳的人。所以这对查理来说真的很有挑战性，他甚至无法与邻居谈论他的愿景。因此，我们看到了许多叙事和倡导现状的小型运动。为了改变法律，卡米尔不得不亲自建立一个由非富裕房主组成的人员联盟。所以，她走进社区，为自己召集了一个看起来更像波特兰的指导委员会。对我来说，这是一个视觉上的挑战，

43
00:23:47.000 --> 00:24:11.000
to be in that room and see so clearly. Yes, there are older white folks with privilege sitting on one side of the room. There's developers huddled in the corner, scheming, and then there's these housing advocates that represent such a diverse kind of illustration of Portland. It took a lot of work and a lot of politics, but the bill passed in 2020.
在那个房间里看得如此清楚。是的，房间的一边坐着有特权的老年白人。开发商蜷缩在角落里，密谋着，然后是这些代表波特兰多样性缩影的住房倡导者。这需要大量的工作和政治手段，但该法案在2020年通过了。

44
00:24:11.000 --> 00:24:50.000
It ended single family zoning and allowed things like cottage clusters and small apartment buildings almost everywhere. If you want to tear down an old relic of a house and turn it into a fourplex, you can do that now. Congratulations, whether it used to be one home, now there are four. You've created some density, made some money, and today Portland is an oasis of affordable, missing middle housing. Right, Camille? I wish I could say that, Jay. Oh, damn, never mind. So why not? What's the hold up? Where's Portland's missing middle? The problem, I think, with our process is, yes, zoning codes go into effect, but the interpretation
它结束了单户住宅分区，并允许在几乎任何地方建造小屋群和小型公寓楼。如果你想拆掉一栋旧房子，把它变成四联排公寓，你现在可以这样做。恭喜，无论它过去是一个家，现在有四个了。你创造了一些密度，赚了一些钱，今天波特兰成了经济适用的“缺失的中产住宅”的绿洲。对吧，卡米尔？我希望我能这么说，杰伊。哦，该死，没关系。那为什么不呢？阻碍是什么？波特兰的“缺失的中产住宅”在哪里？我认为我们流程的问题在于，是的，分区法规生效了，但开发商，甚至是非营利性开发商、社区驱动的开发商对该分区法规的解读，

45
00:24:50.000 --> 00:25:38.000
of that zoning code by developers, even nonprofit developers, community driven developers, means that there's a lot of calculations at play. How much is the cost of land? Can we actually create these things? And so what I would have loved to see is more incentives to actualize what is being proposed or what was proposed. While zoning reform isn't a silver bullet for a housing crisis, it is a big first step. American cities like Minneapolis and Seattle have also recently banned exclusive single-family zoning to address this problem head on. There is a different way of planning a city like Toronto. Namiblanda has a vision. One that more closely resembles the European cities I'm familiar with.
意味着有很多计算在起作用。土地成本是多少？我们真的能创造这些东西吗？所以我很想看到更多的激励措施来实现所提议或曾经提议的内容。虽然分区改革不是解决住房危机的灵丹妙药，但它是重要的一大步。明尼阿波利斯和西雅图等美国城市最近也禁止了排他性的单户住宅分区，以直接解决这个问题。规划像多伦多这样的城市有另一种方式。娜玛·布隆德有一个愿景。一个更接近我熟悉的欧洲城市的愿景。

46
00:25:38.000 --> 00:26:17.000
I moved to Toronto eight years ago. And one of my first strongest impressions of the city was I took the subway, I got out and steps away from the subway. There are neighborhoods of single-family houses, sea of single-family houses. And for someone who, you know, I wasn't born in North America, it's a strange feeling. You just don't get it, right? It's you're in the downtown or steps away from the subway and you have this suburban feeling of instead of an urban feeling, right?
